\fs56 \cf2 \cb3 Effort level: how hard Claude thinks
\fs64 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 By default, Claude reasons through problems before answering. You'll see hints like "still thinking" while it works. If you want to see Claude's reasoning process, press 
\f4\b \cf4 Ctrl+O
\f2\b0 \cf2  to expand the actual reasoning steps.\cb1 \
\cb3 You can control is how Claude reasons through a problem by setting an 
\f3\b effort level
\f2\b0 . Run 
\f4\b \cf4 /effort
\f2\b0 \cf2  to see your current level and adjust it: 
\f4\b \cf4 low
\f2\b0 \cf2  is faster and cheaper, 
\f4\b \cf4 max
\f2\b0 \cf2  reasons longest on hard problems. The default depends on your model and plan \'97 
\f4\b \cf4 /effort
\f2\b0 \cf2  shows you what yours is.\cb1 \
\cb3 If you want to signal to Claude that it should do extra thinking on a single prompt, use the keyword 
\f4\b \cf4 ultrathink
\f2\b0 \cf2  in your prompt. This signals to Claude that it should reason more on this turn, but doesn't adjust the session's effort level.\cb1 \
